ced  a lot of people in the north that  african americans were willing to fight  for their freedom  it's hard to imagine but at that point a  lot of people questioned whether they  would  and that's one of the major  contributions of smalls is that he  became this  focal point for people to see that  african americans were willing to do  whatever for their freedom to take any  chance and they would fight for the  union so i think it was it was a  combination of a lot of things but i  think certainly small's role was was  definitely important in  in changing that so when you began to  research uh for this book where'd you go  national archives great resource um  smalls for years fought for his pension  um because he was not enlisted um of  course he was not enlisted because he  would not have been allowed to be a  pilot at the time but he certainly  served as a member of the union  and should have been  given a  pension for that so he fought for years  so his pension records were very helpful  um at the archives also of course going  to charleston and beaufort i'm going to  some of their archives the state  archives in colombia  talking to a lot of experts  um  you know it's always good to cast a wide  net and see what you can find  there's a man named michael moore  who is the great great grandson of  robert smalls  and here he is doing a ted talk back in  2015.  this is a picture of his house  with the money one of the main things  that he did with his reward money is he  actually bought the house that his  master had  so he bought the big house and so i  always  yeah exactly there's there's great  poetry and you know a former slave going  and buying the big house  that i always agree to but but what also  was i think particularly special after  the war his former master had died  and his former master's wife  who at the time was mentally and  physically ill  came back to the house thinking that it  was still  her house and that she was still  you know the the the woman of the house  and robert embraced her and brought her  in and cared for her through her  remaining days he even allowed her to  sort of stay in the master bedroom where  she had always lived thinking that she  was still the woman of the house  do you have any idea why  robert smalls did that for mrs mcgee  robert smalls was an incredibly  forgiving uh man and he was um a  brilliant man and he um  would always surprise people and so you  know this is one of these um stories  that you know michael bulwarmor and his  mother helen bullermore have been so  incredibly supportive of my project with  this book  but it's interesting when you hear  stories from the family and then you go  and try to verify of course whenever you  possibly can  this is a story i had heard several  times about smalls bringing the family  to his home  the best documentation i could find for  it was one of the missionaries who had  come from the north to help the former  slaves in in port royal  wrote about it and she said that robert  had in the 1870s paid for their uh the  mckee family's  rail fair to charleston and allowed them  to stay in the home  the story that you'll hear on the  carriage rides through buford a lot of  times is that she lived there for the  rest of her life my understanding from  laura townes diary is that it was for a  short amount of time the family came  it's still extraordinary that robert  smalls would allow  the family to come back to the home  where he had once been enslaved he  helped pay for their he paid for their  trip to the house  and he ended up when the family refused  to eat with him he  ended up serving them separately in  their own table so  um  he was just a remarkable man i don't  know too many people who would have done  that now um some people you know say  whether well was he a mckee family  member you know the robert's father is  not known  um i tend to think  smalls was not mckee's child only  because  lydia his mother was already was raising  henry mckee for so long  but regardless of that smalls was an  incredibly warm caring person who wanted  to help people and he did not let  what he had been through during the war  stop him from helping the mckee family  he ended up i think helping  one of the the mckees sons get into  college and  he did some extraordinary things  the uh michael moore runs the the  international african-american museum in  charleston yes what's it like  uh well it has it's being built right  now so um they're raising funds for it  but it sounds phenomenal  and he's the perfect person to lead it  and i recently met with him and asked  him what is it like to walk through  these streets of charleston  knowing that your great great  grandfather was enslaved in this city  and you know it's surreal for him at  many times but it's it's such an honor i  think to um  keep small's memory alive i mean he  michael and his mother have done a great  job of preserving small's legacy uh his  mother created a traveling exhibit  they  are very uh concerned with with  protecting small's name and reputation  and getting getting his story out there  the chair of the university of texas uh  history department back in 2012 was  marvin delaney here he is talking about  another period right after the civil war  and i want you to expound on this  the general  consensus in in 1909 early 20th century  in fact almost up up to the 1960s was  that reconstruction was one of the worst  periods  in the history of this country  that reconstruction was characterized by  corrupt  carpetbaggers  and scallywags  and ignorant negroes  who misruled the south  the  so-called ignorant negroes who had  served in the governments in the south  during this period had made three very  important contributions  they had brought about the first  democratic governments in the south  they had established the first free  public schools  and they had passed new social  legislation  and as many of you know robert smalls  did all three of those things  what was reconstruction and how long did  it last  well a lot of people don't realize that  reconstruction sort of began with the  port royal experiment which was the  efforts to help these 10 000 abandoned  slaves  in in port royal and the beaufort area  because reconstruction was  the country trying to rebuild itself  after the war  and  having to incorporate  slaves into the our our whole world as  free people i mean these were people who  were kept from  any education they had never been  allowed to care for themselves and  suddenly they were free and what does  that mean  so um you know buford  is really in many ways the heart of  reconstruction um because this is where  the port royal experiment happened um  and then of course after the war robert  moved back to beaufort um  and really ended up finding a voice in  politics and um and uh it was at a time  when a lot of um  blacks were being uh voted into  political power and he was he took  advantage of that and um  ended up doing a lot i mean he actually  helped set up the first free and  compulsory education system in south  carolina um so it was a time of  rebuilding it was the time where we were  trying to  figure out how we were going to move  forward after the legacy of slavery  which of course we're still trying to do  today did he ever get any education  yes education was huge for robert small  so during the war um he went to  philadelphia for several months while it  was the planner was being refurbished he  was charged with going up there and and  seeing to um watching over the  refurbishment and during that time he  took advantage of the extra time he had  and hired a tutor  um then it's it's thought that  throughout his life he actually worked  with other tutors so he was doing the  best to educate himself and then as his  children grew up he sent them to the  best schools that he possibly could  in fact when his daughter elizabeth who  was on board the planner when he escaped  when she was 13 years old she read  the declaration of independence at a  fourth of july celebration in beaufort  and to the audience was mostly former  slaves  and it was a huge moment because for  them they saw that  this was an opportunity  their children were now going to learn  to read and write their children were  going to be educated they had features  that they never could have imagined  before so education was wasn't critical  to robert smalls  life you said that he was five terms in  the u.s house of representatives a  member of the republican party at the  time what's the bribery story  well uh smalls was elected in 1875 to  his first term in the house of  representatives and two years later  he was accused of  of taking funds while in a state  legislative role  it was i think it was a time  it was a time when people were scared  scared of blacks having more power there  was a lot going on and i think this was  the charges were an attempt to  to ruin his credibility  he was eventually pardoned but it was  not before he was sentenced to three  years hard labor and his conviction was  basically um based on a felon's  testimony did he serve for the hard  labor for three years no he served three  days in jail but was  pardoned uh in part because the south  carolina governor created a deal  that robert smalls wanted no part of he  actually  wanted the court case to go to the  supreme court where he could have his  say and prove his name prove his  innocence  but was never given that opportunity  because a deal was made in order to  i think free some  i think it was some red shirts which  were the name for these very um  white  racist fanatics in the south at the time  at the time  there were a number of blacks elected as  republicans from the south in  reconstruction when did that all end  when did when did  when did reconstruction fall apart  it's a very good question um  it's not my area of expertise um  but um i would say that you know we saw  the height of the power in the 1870s  1880s and then after that it it started  changing one of the things that i found  so shocking in my research is i did not  understand that the lengths that that  the southern states had gone to all to  reinstate slavery and all but name after  the war um so there was a lot of  violence going on small certainly  faced numerous death threats throughout  his political career  he had a unique position in the fact  that he was because his mother had grown  up on a plantation and spoke gullah  which was the local  language of  all the slaves from west africa had to  have a language that they all understood  and this became gullah  he could talk with a lot of the former  slaves in south carolina and he could  talk to the whites who were trying to  regain their power  so he acted often as a bridge between  the white and black communities but it  was it was a it was a tough road and as  we saw you know with the  jim crow laws and everything else we we  had a lot of backsliding  stephen weiss curator uh at parris  islands museum uh  somebody you i know interviewed for your  book and also he has written about it  but here he is talking about robert  smalls  uh two individuals that are associated  with the sea islands around port royal  robert smalls obviously a native another  individual who comes down and is at penn  center to help plan his strategy in the  1960s  both are very articulate both are very  well known for their speeches if nothing  else at least a lot of robert smalls of  speeches are written down and they're  wonderful things he has wonderful sense  of humor and the 1895 convention and  such when he  makes  comments back at pittsford ben tillman  and others as well  both are extremely brave  robert smalls never went around armed  after the civil war there were threats  against him he once commented uh when  you face down an eight-inch confederate  cannon these little pistols mean nothing  to you  and both were pacifists  how did stephen wise fit in all this  stephen weiss is the most knowledgeable  person about the civil war charleston  beaufort area that you'll ever meet he  and larry rowland wrote a three-volume  history of beaufort  which is fascinating and has everything  you could ever want to know about the  area in it  but steve uh was a great resource he and  larry  read the manuscript over gave insights  gave feedback pointed me to  the right people to talk to  incredibly helpful throughout the whole  process whatever happened to the planter  well  yeah unfortunately in the 1870s she  ended up sinking long after smalls had  said goodbye to her  after the war when steamers weren't  needed as much  the union actually put her up for  auction  and interestingly enough  ferguson who was the original owner  wanted the planner back he was  determined to have it i think it was  symbolic to him in some way and he was a  southerner he was a southerner  and he  he tried to get it back the first time  the boat went up for auction but he the  government figured out who he was and  that he had leased the ship to the  confederates and they turned him down  but we think that he actually had  someone else broker the deal for him  when the ship went up for auction in  baltimore a few months later  so  in 1866  ferguson had the ship back 1866 1867  and then about 10 years later  the ship was trying to tow another ship  and she sank  and  parts of the planner were salvaged at  the time but she was obviously no longer  in operation  and smalls was said to have said when he  learned  that it was like losing a member of his  family  speaking of family what happened to his  wife hannah how long did she live and  she was 13 years older than he was right  right and unfortunately hannah died  fairly young  and they had of course two children that  lived they had a third a young son who  died during the war  but a few years later i think about  seven years later smalls married another  woman a schoolteacher from charleston  annie wigg and he had a son with her as  well  so he had two wives i think hannah often  is overlooked as a hero in the story  unfortunately we don't have a lot of  records on hannah  i could only find one  newspaper source that actually recorded  anything from hannah and she was talking  about how they had agreed if it looked  like they were going to be captured when  when if they were attacked when they  were escaping that they would all hold  hands and jump overboard and drown  rather than be  recaptured and taken back to slavery  you say that  mr smalls died when he was 75 what was  the rest of his life like after being in  the united states congress  and uh what was the impact of the  bribery accusation  yeah i don't think he ever fully  recovered from that  accusation uh he ended up being  re-elected  but he it was never quite the same he  ended up becoming a customs collector in  beaufort and spent many spent many years  doing that but i think he really wanted  to be back in d.c  making change making people's lives  better  but unfortunately that was not the case  but he had a nice life he um of course  as we saw from the video earlier he  ended up being able to buy the home that  he had once been enslaved in and that  was so important to him and he you know  one of my favorite quotes of his is um  something about how he was so happy to  be able to sen to give that to leave  that house for his children and his  family what did he die of  uh diet complications from diabetes and  where is he buried he's buried in  beaufort at the tabernacle church which  he was a member of in the last years of  his life they have a nice statue there  and he's buried beside his two wives and  one of his daughters  in your opinion has the united states  paid enough  honor to him for what he did i don't  think so i think there's a recognition  that's starting to happen  about his contributions in in charleston  there are two markers now historic  markers  in downtown charleston that talk about  the planter and marks robert smalls  contributions  but i think with this renewed interest  in reconstruction um  you know there are several places in  beaufort that are not part of a  reconstruction monument now  um there's more of a i think  charlestonians in general and the whole  country is embracing more of its  african-american history and in doing  that i think robert smalls will get the  recognition that he deserves  the name of the book is be free or die  the amazing story of robert smalls  escape from slavery to union hero  and our guest kate lineberry thank you  very much for joining us thank you so  much  [Music]  do  [Music]  for free transcripts or to give us your  comments about this program visit us at  qna.org q a programs are also available  as c-span podcasts  [Music]  if you enjoyed this week's q a interview  with kate lineberry here are some other  programs you might like author clarence  lussane talks about his book the black  history of the white house history  professor edna mefford on the life and  career of abraham lincoln and historian  ronald white discusses his biography of  president grant titled american ulysses  you can watch these anytime or search  our entire video library at cspan.org  c-span's washington journal
 

